{
  "concept": "For two-slit interference, the only thing that determines whether light arriving at a point P on the screen interferes constructively or destructively is the tiny extra path length Δl = d·sinθ travelled by the far slit's ray, not the individual path lengths r₁ and r₂.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Panel (a) label beneath the left construction; panel (b) label beneath the right construction",
    "Left panel: vertical tan/ochre bar representing the slit barrier with two gaps",
    "Slit labels S₁ (upper) and S₂ (lower) with small black tick marks",
    "Vertical double-headed arrow bracket on the far left labeled d spanning S₁ to S₂",
    "Gray vertical bar on the right labeled 'Screen'",
    "Horizontal double-headed arrow bracket below labeled D spanning barrier to screen",
    "Teal ray r₁ from S₁ to point P with mid-ray arrowhead and label r₁",
    "Teal ray r₂ from S₂ to point P with mid-ray arrowhead and label r₂",
    "Short sinusoidal wave trains drawn on each teal ray near the slits",
    "Black filled dot on the screen labeled P",
    "Dashed horizontal center line from the slit midpoint to the screen, ending at labeled point C",
    "Dashed straight line from slit midpoint to P (the bisector ray)",
    "Angle arc labeled θ between the dashed horizontal and the dashed midpoint-to-P line",
    "Vertical double-headed arrow bracket on the right labeled y spanning C up to P",
    "Right panel: same tan barrier bar with two slit gaps, vertical bracket labeled d",
    "Two parallel teal rays leaving the slits at angle θ, each with an arrowhead and the label 'to P'",
    "Dashed horizontal reference lines from each slit with angle arcs labeled θ",
    "Dashed segment from the upper slit dropped perpendicular onto the lower ray, with a right-angle square marker",
    "Dashed segment from the upper slit continuing to close the small right triangle",
    "Short double-headed arrow along the lower ray labeled Δl marking the extra path length",
    "Readout annotation showing current values of y, θ, d, Δl and Δl/λ (order m)",
    "Constructive/destructive indicator text tied to whether Δl is a whole or half number of wavelengths"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "pointHeight",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "Moving the observation point P up the screen increases the angle θ, which in turn lengthens the extra path Δl — so position on the screen and path difference are two views of the same quantity.",
      "label": "Height of P on screen, y (mm)",
      "range": [-20, 20, 0.5],
      "default": 8
    },
    {
      "id": "slitSeparation",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "For a fixed viewing angle, widening the slits scales the extra path length in direct proportion, making Δl = d·sinθ visible as a geometric fact rather than a formula.",
      "label": "Slit separation d (µm)",
      "range": [5, 60, 1],
      "default": 25
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "The full apparatus shows two long, slightly unequal paths; the near-slit magnification shows that their entire difference is captured by one short right-triangle leg — the far-field approximation that makes interference tractable.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Full apparatus (a)", "Near-slit zoom (b)", "Both side by side"],
      "default": "Both side by side"
    },
    {
      "id": "hoverLabels",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Each geometric quantity (d, D, y, θ, r₁, r₂, Δl) names a specific segment or angle in the construction, so the symbols in the formula map onto real pieces of the picture."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The apparatus",
      "narration": "Light leaves two slits separated by d and travels a distance D to the screen. Choose a point P a height y above the center point C; the rays r₁ and r₂ reach it along slightly different paths.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Full apparatus (a)", "pointHeight": 8, "slitSeparation": 25 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide P up the screen",
      "narration": "Drag P upward. The angle θ between the center line and the line to P grows, and r₁ becomes noticeably shorter than r₂ — but the difference is far too small to see at this scale.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Full apparatus (a)", "pointHeight": 18, "slitSeparation": 25 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Zoom in on the slits",
      "narration": "Because D is enormous compared with d, the two rays to P are essentially parallel. Dropping a perpendicular from the upper slit onto the lower ray isolates the whole difference in one short segment, Δl.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Near-slit zoom (b)", "pointHeight": 18, "slitSeparation": 25 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the right triangle",
      "narration": "The triangle has hypotenuse d and the angle at the slit equals θ, so the isolated leg is exactly Δl = d·sinθ. Watch it shrink as you move P back toward the center.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Near-slit zoom (b)", "pointHeight": 3, "slitSeparation": 25 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Widen the slits",
      "narration": "Hold the angle and widen d: Δl grows in direct proportion, so more bright fringes are packed into the same span of screen.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Near-slit zoom (b)", "pointHeight": 8, "slitSeparation": 55 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Both views at once",
      "narration": "Side by side, a screen-scale change in y and a micron-scale change in Δl move together. Whenever Δl is a whole number of wavelengths the waves arrive in phase and P is bright; a half-integer gives darkness.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Both side by side", "pointHeight": 12, "slitSeparation": 25 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The kilometre-long journey of each ray is irrelevant — only the microscopic leftover leg Δl = d·sinθ, cut off by a perpendicular right at the slits, decides whether P is bright or dark.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two constructions side by side on a white background, each with a small italic panel label (a) and (b) centered beneath. Left panel: a vertical tan/ochre bar at the left edge is the slit barrier with two small gaps labeled S₁ (upper) and S₂ (lower); a thin vertical double-arrow bracket to their left is labeled d. A gray vertical bar at the right is the screen, labeled 'Screen' at its top. A horizontal double-arrow bracket below spans barrier to screen, labeled D. Two teal rays rise from the slits to a black dot labeled P high on the screen, each with a mid-ray arrowhead and italic labels r₁ (upper) and r₂ (lower); short teal sine wave trains ride on each ray just past the slits. A black dashed horizontal runs from the slit midpoint to the screen, meeting it at the labeled point C, with a right-hand vertical double-arrow bracket labeled y from C up to P. A dashed line from the slit midpoint to P makes a small angle arc labeled θ with the dashed horizontal. Right panel: the same tan barrier and two slits with a d bracket at the left, but the two teal rays are now drawn parallel, each rising at the same angle θ, each ending in an arrowhead with the text 'to P'. Dashed horizontal reference lines emerge from each slit with θ arcs. A dashed line from the upper slit meets the lower ray at a small square right-angle marker; the short stretch of the lower ray between that foot and the lower slit is bracketed with a double arrow and labeled Δl, with an additional dashed segment closing the small right triangle below. Keep rays teal, barrier tan, screen gray, construction lines thin black dashes, labels serif italic. Add a compact live readout box (y, θ, d, Δl, Δl/λ) and a bright/dark indicator that changes color as Δl crosses integer and half-integer multiples of the wavelength. Δl should be drawn exaggerated for legibility while the readout reports honest physical values."
}

